  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-253900",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $y$ for any $y>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
